        ":question/id": "ae335859-0dfa-58d7-a7ca-cf642f79ca2b",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $a=⟨1,2,3⟩,$ $b=⟨-1,0,-2⟩,$ and $c=⟨0,-2,1⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2))i-((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))j+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-4i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4+2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
