        ":question/problem": "Given that $a=⟨-2,4,3⟩,$ $b=⟨-1,3,2⟩,$ and $c=⟨2,-1,4⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)j+((-1)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =14i+8j-5k \\\\  & =⟨14,8,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨14,8,-5⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 14+4\\cdot 8+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
